diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
index 2294f62..a582d90 100644
--- a/docs/bots.md
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -37,3 +37,37 @@ curl -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished" \
   `Message#broadcast_create` is called and the model's
   `after_create_commit -> room.receive` hook enqueues push notifications,
   exactly as for a human-authored message.
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+- `users.webhook_url` is a bot's optional HTTP callback, edited from the
+  same bot form as its name/avatar ("Webhook URL" on `/bots/new` and the
+  edit page). Saving the form with the field blank removes the webhook
+  (`Bot` normalizes blank values to `NULL`).
+- Bots appear in the @-mention autocomplete exactly like people (they are
+  users, room members, and `Autocompletable::UsersController` already
+  returns active room members). Only a bot that belongs to the room can
+  be a mentionee (`Message::Mentionee`), so only room members' bots are
+  notified.
+- `Message::BotMentions` runs `after_create_commit`: for every mentioned
+  bot with a webhook URL it enqueues `BotWebhookDeliveryJob`. Doing this
+  in a job means talking to a bot's webhook (timeouts, slow or broken
+  servers) never slows down the person posting the message. Messages
+  authored by bots themselves never enqueue deliveries, so a webhook
+  reply (or bot-to-bot chatter) cannot loop back into webhooks.
+- The job POSTs `Content-Type: application/json` with `event: "mention"`,
+  the author (`id`, `name`), the room (`id`, `name`, and `reply_to` —
+  that bot's own `/rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` posting URL), and
+  the message (`id`, `body`, `link`). `body.text` is the plain-text body
+  with the bot's own `@-mention` stripped (other mentions remain), so it
+  reads as the command addressed to the bot; `body.html` is the full
+  rich-text HTML. Paths (`reply_to`, `link`) are relative — the bot is
+  expected to know its Campfire origin. `link` is
+  `/rooms/:room_id/@:message_id`, which opens the room at that message.
+- Replies: `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html` posts
+  the response body into the room as the bot (created exactly like an
+  asynchronously-posted bot message, then broadcast). `204`, any other
+  status/content type, a blank body, or any network/timeout failure
+  posts nothing and is only logged — webhook failures never affect the
+  original message. A bot that wants to reply later (or not
+  synchronously) returns `204` and POSTs to `reply_to` when ready.
